  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Centre for Humanitarian Data Phase 3 (2023-27)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Centre&#x2019;s operations are organized around four workstreams: data services, data science, data responsibility, and learning and practice. Together, the workstreams contribute to the Centre&#x2019;s four objectives: 1) Increase the availability and use of quality data for humanitarian response (data services); 2. Increase the application of data science and risk analysis to support preemptive decision making (data science); 3) Increase trust and accountability across organizations sharing and using data (data responsibility); and 4) Increase the confidence of humanitarians to work with data (learning and practice).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The mission of the Centre is to work with partners to increase the use and impact of data in humanitarian response. The Centre&#x2019;s theory of change is that accessible and reliable data, supported by a network of skilled users, will lead to greater and more effective use of data in humanitarian crises. This, in turn, underpins an assumption that data use is a building block for making decisions that better reflect the needs and circumstances of people in crises. In other words, data has a vital role to play in improving humanitarian outcomes.</narrative>
  </description>
